Id: fsys021w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_wfile
Fix: Find them with find DIR -xdev -type f -perm -o+w and chmod o-w the ones that should not be shared, or set Tiger_FSScan_DetailDirs to list them.
References: find(1); chmod(1); tigerrc
Controls: CIS 3.3; NIST AC-3; ISO 8.3; CE Secure configuration

World-writable files were found in a directory outside the system
trees (home directories, caches, /tmp, data disks), where one is usually
a tool's lock or cache file made with mode 666 (Hugging Face's hub, a
desktop updater's cache) rather than a way in, so Tigris reports how
many and where instead of listing each. The directories where each file
is still listed (fsys020f) are set by Tiger_FSScan_DetailDirs in
tigerrc; set it to '*' to list them all. Whoever can write one of these
files can change what the account that reads it sees or runs, so a
world-writable shell start-up file or script in a home directory is
worth fixing; a lock file is not.
